  "question": {
    "text": "The temperature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mfenced><mi>t</mi></mfenced></math> of a body at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>160</mn><mo>°</mo></msup><mo> </mo><mi>F</mi></math> and it decreases continuously as per the differential equation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mfenced><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>80</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> is positive constant. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mfenced><mn>15</mn></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>°</mo></msup><mo> </mo><mi>F</mi></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi><mfenced><mn>45</mn></mfenced></math> is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
